"Keep the map dead overhead and north-up. Use one parallel oblique projection for everything drawn on it. Ground contact points stay on the source plan. For a point above ground, start at its own plan position and shift it straight up the page by 0.60 times its physical height at local ground scale. Equal heights use equal offsets everywhere. Lay out each roof, landing and bridge at its own elevation. Horizontal planes retain their plan shapes and orientation; parallels stay parallel without a vanishing point. Join matching structural points across levels. Trace the near lip of an opening, its inner return and the far surface visible through it. Draw from back to front, letting a nearer eave hide part of a gallery. Use line weight, hatching and contact shade beneath overhangs to distinguish the layers. Use a 1.7-metre adult and 2.1-metre doorway at the same scale throughout. Carry a believable storey spacing up the occupied wings. Show larger spans where a performance hall, aviary or instrument actually needs them. Prioritise mapped building footprints for the principal architecture. Distribute related activities across the usable parcels; align secondary benches, planting beds and installations with narrower strips. A raised roof may hide an amber street behind the building. Keep the street aligned where it reappears on both sides. Leave foreground streets visible and finish every roof and rear corner inside the safety margin. Draw a hand-coloured architectural atlas miniature in fine sepia ink on warm paper. At map zoom, make the site legible from its silhouette, open spaces and pattern of roofs. At native resolution let the eye discover who works here, what they handle and how they move through the place. Use the museum garden as a reference for composition: a path leads from the entrance past beds and glasshouses to specimen courts. Use the tidal observatory for architectural depth: curved copper-green wings enclose a brass pendulum, with galleries behind it and stepped basins below. Use the kite yard for human scale and colour: indigo and vermilion wings fill the yard while seated makers, bamboo spars, pigment bowls and drying cloth explain how the wings are made. Translate those relationships into the requested subject. Choose pigments from the materials of this site. Warm stone, inked timber and slate can frame expanses of signature colour such as patinated turquoise metal or patterned silk. Draw glazing ribs on glass, joints in stone, folds in fabric and wear at thresholds. Concentrate fine marks around use and construction; give the adjacent roof or court enough quiet space for that activity to read."
